      "writtenOn": "2025-11-20T19:24:03Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Hmm, this change might be significantly better than I first thought. The use case mentioned in the commit message is not a very good one, it is a fetch of a branch that was already fetched as FETCH_HEAD, but since it didn\u0027t get saved as a publicly visible ref, it took a while for the negotiation process to identify it as a NOP, but it was a NOP because the usual progress output did not appear. Interesting that it even improved this use case at all! Perhaps it helps smooth over a bunch of the sporadic IO?\n\nIt a proper fetch, this does seem to really help the receiving phase, I can tell that the CPU usage jumps back and forth between around the 150s to 250s, versus before this change, the CPU would jump from around the 40s to 140s. On close fetches, this is taking a fetch down from around 4m14s to 3m59s. But across the ocean, it is even better, it takes a fetch down from around 6m10s to 5m30s!!! I think the CPU usage is a bit more consistent when done across the ocean, which makes sense since there is probably more waiting time due to the network latencies. So instead of the async helping by offloading CPU to another thread, it likely is helping more by just insulating the local IO from the network blocking, it allows all resources to be better used at the same time, smoothing out the bursts.",
      "parentUuid": "13010ec9_15151fa7",
      "revId": "d322afc18989084f4d61f829c6c1a7488074e6a4",
      "serverId": "173816e5-2b9a-37c3-8a2e-48639d4f1153"
    },
